    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1573">

                <pb facs="1001" n="885" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg885"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.62/43.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Consul Hostetter to the Secretary of State.</hi><anchor
                        n="148" xml:id="d1573-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Hermosillo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-02-01">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 1, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s January 31,7 p, m. Carranza claims German Minister’s charges are
                    absurd as no such orders were ever issued. Orders have been issued to respect
                    life and property of all foreigners, all nationalities included. Some foreigners
                    have mixed in politics and these may have suffered some inconvenience but none
                    are molested who remain neutral. He has asked for information and will advise as
                    soon as received.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hostetter</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1573-f1" n="148" type="source" xml:id="d1573-f1-content">
                <p>Communicated to the American Chargé d’Affaires at Mexico City February 2,
                    1914.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
